you were growing up quapa

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.260
or, sorry, 'cause you're a quarter, right?

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.550
- Oh, oh, that's what it's called?

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.468
- Oh, yeah. (chuckles)
- That's good to know.

00:05:07.468 --> 00:05:09.210
- I have a friend that's quapa.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.770
I guess like, what was that like for you?

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.870
As far as I know, as biracial person,

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.400
it's like you feel like an imposter

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.750
in your own culture sometimes.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:16.590
- Yeah, definitely.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.350
I mean, I'm the lightest
of me and my siblings.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.900
I have a younger sister, who's
like the color of Rhianna,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.610
and then my brother is super tan.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.520
And so, then there's me,
white girl with a giant Afro,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.697
and people are always like,

00:05:27.697 --> 00:05:29.790
"How is your hair so frizzy?"

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.890
I'm like, "Well, it's
curly. It's an Afro."

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:34.590
You know, explaining to people who I am.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.790
It was hard at times.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.900
But then I also was really proud

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.820
that I got to say like,
"Oh, I'm part Black.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:39.930
That's why I have this cool hair."

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.330
But it took me a while
to really love who I am.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.670
- Have the rest of you
guys felt like that too?

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.320
- One thing I felt is like,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.660
I've actually been to
India multiple times,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.240
and whenever I go, I'm
the lightest one there.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.000
So I just don't feel
Indian at all, really.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.620
- He said that he had been to India,

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.420
and he just kinda threw that out there

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.400
and that felt really quick.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.220
And just like a, but there
wasn't any detail to the story.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.440
- Yeah, for me, being from Colorado,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.950
my experience has always being
too Asian to be white here

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.220
in Colorado, but here at
UCLA, where I'm at right now,

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.300
where it's a lot more diverse,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.330
I'm feeling like sometimes
I'm too white to be Asian.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.390
So no matter what, I always
feel exclusion from one side.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.507
And I think that's one of the struggles

00:06:19.507 --> 00:06:22.380
or one of the experiences
of being biracial.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.213
- I don't know.

00:06:23.213 --> 00:06:24.046
I think when I was younger,

00:06:24.046 --> 00:06:25.530
I was a little more ambiguous looking,

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:26.640
like people would kind of joke

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:27.810
I was like a chameleon growing up

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.730
'cause I didn't look the
same every couple of years.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.520
But it was like, I would
always get made fun of a lot

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.890
by other Korean students
who would say like,

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.320
there's like slurs for biracial people.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:38.527
And so it was always like,

00:06:38.527 --> 00:06:40.140
"Oh, people like you
just shouldn't exist,"

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.280
and stuff like that.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.690
- I'm so sorry.
- Yeah.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.205
- Still here. (chuckles)

00:06:44.205 --> 00:06:45.038
(group chuckles)

00:06:45.038 --> 00:06:46.860
- I grew up in predominantly
white neighborhoods

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:47.763
my whole life.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.630
And I don't speak Spanish.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.520
I don't speak Navajo.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.500
I never had a quinceañera, (titters)

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.200
so I'm not really like a
hundred percent in each culture.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.120
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.280
- For me, growing up
mostly with my mom's family

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.630
who's majority white,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.670
and then my dad's family
lives farther away.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.530
So, soul food and all that stuff,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.700
I kinda missed out on,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.860
and I got to kinda explore later in life.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:12.889
But that's something
that I always wish I had.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:14.169
(timer ticking)

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:15.002
- I'm glad we all get to talk.

00:07:15.002 --> 00:07:16.312
- Yeah, that was a good discussion.

00:07:16.312 --> 00:07:17.207
(timer rings)

00:07:17.207 --> 00:07:19.947
(upbeat music)

00:07:19.947 --> 00:07:22.470
